export const title = 'Value and events';
export const order = 200;
---

<p>
  The value is a <code>DocumentValue</code>: the page setup and three Tiptap
  JSON documents. JSON keeps everything the editor can show, which Markdown
  cannot: alignment, text color, page size, margins, page breaks, and the
  header and footer. Store it as is, and set it back to load the document.
</p>

<pre><code>{'{'}
  version: 1,
  page: {'{'} size: 'a4', orientation: 'portrait',
          margins: {'{'} top: 25.4, right: 25.4, bottom: 25.4, left: 25.4 {'}'} {'}'},  // millimetres
  header: {'{'} type: 'doc', content: [...] {'}'},
  footer: {'{'} type: 'doc', content: [...] {'}'},
  body:   {'{'} type: 'doc', content: [...] {'}'},
{'}'}</code></pre>

<p>
  <code>input</code> fires with <code>{'{ value }'}</code> as the document
  changes, <code>debounce</code> milliseconds after typing stops (400 by
  default), and <code>change</code> when focus leaves after an edit. A value
  set on the editor loads at once.
</p>

<h3 class="text-title-medium">Exports</h3>
<ul>
  <li>
    <code>getMarkdown()</code> returns the body as Markdown, for search
    indexes, notifications or a <code>wc-markdown</code> preview. Alignment,
    colors, the header and footer are left out; page breaks become
    <code>&lt;!-- page break --&gt;</code>.
  </li>
  <li><code>getHTML()</code> returns the body as HTML, for email.</li>
  <li>
    <code>importMarkdown(markdown)</code> replaces the body with Markdown, for
    example to turn a <code>wc-markdown-editor</code> description into a
    document.
  </li>
  <li>
    <code>print()</code> prints the pages exactly as they look on screen,
    with the header, footer and page numbers, on white paper whatever the
    theme.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3 class="text-title-medium">Images</h3>
<p>
  Adding an image (the toolbar button, a paste or a drop) fires
  <code>image-upload</code> with the <code>file</code> and a
  <code>respond(src, alt?)</code> function. Store the file and call
  <code>respond</code> with its address; the image is inserted where it was
  added. Peacock never uploads files and never embeds them in the value. Only
  web and relative addresses are accepted. Images can be resized by dragging
  their corners.
</p>

<pre><code>editor.addEventListener('image-upload', async event =&gt; {'{'}
  const {'{ file, respond }'} = event.detail;
  const url = await uploadToStorage(file);   // your storage
  respond(url, file.name);
{'}'});</code></pre>

<h3 class="text-title-medium">Mentions</h3>
<p>
  Mentions work as on <code>wc-markdown-editor</code>: answer
  <code>search</code> by setting <code>suggestions</code>.
  <code>triggers=""</code> turns them off.
</p>
